Grid Engine backend
===================

This backend targets Altair Grid Engine 2024.1 (engine 8.9.0) and retains
compatibility with Sun, Oracle and Univa-derived Grid Engine installations that
provide qsub/qstat/qacct/qdel/qconf/qhost and the established XML interfaces.

Required site setup
-------------------

Set `lrms=sge`, make `SGE_ROOT` available through `sge_root` or the service
environment and, when the commands are outside PATH, set `sge_bin_path`. The
command directory must contain the complete Grid Engine client suite. ARC
forces the C locale, requests `qsub -terse` for submission (while accepting
the documented ordinary success response a JSV can restore),
`qstat -xml` for live state and `qacct` for terminal accounting.  Direct access
to `$SGE_ROOT/$SGE_CELL/common/accounting` is neither needed nor assumed.
Worker nodes must be able to see the ARC session filesystem; the backend
rejects `shared_filesystem=no` because no SGE-specific file transport exists.
Per-share `sge_queues` mappings contain exact cluster-queue names, not queue
instances or wildcard expressions; ARC uses the same mapping for submission
and information-provider aggregation.

Jobs requesting more than one slot require `sge_pe` (globally or per queue), or
an RTE which adds the name of an installed PE as a nodeproperty.  A distributed
PE must set `control_slaves TRUE` and `accounting_summary TRUE`; otherwise ARC
rejects the job because Grid Engine cannot provide a reliable whole-job resource
summary.  A single-host `$pe_slots` PE can remain loose for threaded workloads.
A tightly integrated PE must also enable summary accounting. A requested
`countpernode` is accepted only when the PE's `allocation_rule` guarantees it.

For physical-memory semantics configure `sge_memory_resource` to a requestable,
per-slot consumable `MEMORY` complex with `<=` relation (commonly `m_mem_free`
on cgroup-enabled Altair Grid Engine sites). ARC auto-selects that complex when available, then
falls back to `h_vmem` for compatibility while warning that it limits virtual
rather than physical memory; the standard non-consumable `h_vmem` definition is
valid for this enforcement-only fallback. Exclusive jobs similarly require the
site-specific requestable, per-slot consumable `BOOL` complex with `EXCL` relation named by
`sge_exclusive_resource`.

Grid Engine buffers accounting writes.  Tune `sge_accounting_retries` and
`sge_wakeupperiod` so their product exceeds the site's `accounting_flush_time`.
The scanner treats migration/rescheduling records (failed codes 24, 25 and 30)
as nonterminal and waits for a later master/summary record.

ARC submits from a private empty directory, starts its ordered request options
with `-clear`, and explicitly restores script interpretation. Global and
passwd-home request defaults which Grid Engine consumes before `-clear` are
rejected when they can create arrays, block synchronously or import another
option file (`-t`, `-tc`, `-binding`, `-sync y` and `-@`).
`sge_jobopts` cannot set options which change the tracked job shape (`-clear`,
`-t`, `-tc`, `-sync`, `-now`, `-b`, `-shell`, `-pe`, `-N` or `-@`); use the dedicated ARC settings
for parallel jobs and the ordinary ARC job-description field for the job name.
ARC also overrides `-u`, `-s` and `-q` from Grid Engine `sge_qstat` default
files. Other qstat defaults are rejected because qstat has no `-clear` option
and a filtered or regrouped snapshot is unsafe for job completion accounting.

Troubleshooting
---------------

Set `sge_debug=yes` in `[lrms]` to trace backend decisions. The scanner writes
to the configured `[arex] helperlog`; submission and cancellation stderr go to
the job's `errors` control file. Apply configuration changes through the normal
A-REX restart procedure: helpers read the runtime configuration generated at
service startup. Messages have UTC timestamps, the helper PID,
`arc_job` and `sge_job`. The initial submission result reports
`assigned_sge_job` before ARC has stored the native ID. Turn tracing off after
collecting a failing pass: it adds per-job messages and command timing calls.
No additional trace files are created, and normal log rotation still applies.
Scripts, GRAMi contents and environment values are not dumped; native command
error text is still reported, so review logs before sharing them.

Start with one ARC job ID and follow these events:

* `environment`: verify the Grid Engine root, cell, binary directory and ports.
  Run manual checks as the same local account as the failing helper. An admin
  shell may use a different cell or different `.sge_request`/`.sge_qstat` files.
* `submit` / `command_result` / `submitted`: correlate the ARC job and assigned
  native ID; a nonzero status is a client failure. Native stderr supplies the
  reason. `parallel_environment` reports the selected PE's allocation and
  accounting settings when a parallel job is being validated.
* `query_start` / `query_result` / `query_retry`: show the read-only command,
  attempt, exit status and elapsed seconds. A missing result after a start
  identifies a command which has not returned. Query failures and malformed
  XML stop the scan without completing jobs.
* `job_snapshot`: compares ARC and scheduler states and submission epochs.
  A live record with a different submission epoch may belong to a reused ID;
  a JSV name rewrite alone does not complete the original job.
* `accounting`: explains `no_matching_job`, `no_master_record`,
  `submission_time_mismatch`, `incomplete_terminal_record` or `selected_master`.
  `accounting_wait` reports the pass counter; `accounting_exhausted` records
  the fallback to wrapper diagnostics. `completed` records the selected
  accounting source and scheduler/wrapper exit values.
* `cancel_result`: a zero exit status means Grid Engine accepted deletion;
  completion still waits for the scanner and accounting.

Useful read-only checks with the same SGE environment (substitute the numeric
native job ID and configured PE/complex names):

    LC_ALL=C TZ=UTC "$SGE_BIN_PATH/qstat" -xml -u '*' -s a -q '*'
    LC_ALL=C "$SGE_BIN_PATH/qstat" -u '*' -s a -q '*' -j JOB_ID
    LC_ALL=C TZ=UTC "$SGE_BIN_PATH/qacct" -j JOB_ID
    LC_ALL=C "$SGE_BIN_PATH/qconf" -sp PE_NAME
    LC_ALL=C "$SGE_BIN_PATH/qconf" -sc

For an error state, inspect `qstat -j`'s error reason and the job errors file
before retrying. ARC records that reason before requesting deletion. For an
accounting delay, compare `sge_accounting_retries * sge_wakeupperiod` with the
site's accounting flush interval and check for master/summary records. Preserve
the helper log excerpt, job errors/diag, ARC and native IDs, client version and
relevant configuration when reporting a problem. Do not rerun submit or cancel
helpers to collect diagnostics: they change scheduler state.

Information-provider reporting and failure contracts
---------------------------------------------------

The active ARC provider is `infoproviders/SGEmod.pm`. `SGE.pm` is the deprecated
ARC0 fallback, not the normal implementation. This reporting review changes the
active provider and the scanner; it does not certify the legacy fallback.

Set `[infosys] loglevel=5` to trace provider queries in the configured information
provider logfile (normally `/var/log/arc/infoprovider.log`). This is separate from
`[lrms] sge_debug=yes`, which traces the submission/scanner/cancellation helpers.
Provider `query_start` and `query_result` messages identify the executable and
arguments, exit status, terminating signal, output byte count and elapsed seconds.
Native stderr remains in the provider log; parser failures identify the command
or document, job, queue and field where applicable. No complete scheduler output
or environment dump is added. Restore the normal log level after diagnosis.

All process launches use explicit argv execution, including commands with no
arguments and executable paths containing spaces. Each child gets the C locale;
loading the module does not change the caller's locale. Collection-specific SGE
cell/port/environment changes are restored on both success and exceptions.

The command runner now waits for successful process exit before invoking parsers.
Nonzero exits and signals discard stdout, even when it resembles valid data.
Missing executables return failure to the caller, rather than exiting inside the
logger. Required inventory/configuration queries abort collection on failure;
optional `qstat -j` failures omit details while retaining the successful live
snapshot. An unrecognizable version banner is explicitly reported as unknown.
No failed collection is returned as a successful empty cluster.

Well-formed XML is not sufficient. The provider and scanner validate the qstat
root, required sections and job identities before accepting the snapshot.
The provider additionally checks owners, slots, queue counters and array ranges
before updating node/job counters. A genuine empty snapshot still succeeds.
Bad qhost capacity values and missing/malformed qconf limits are not silently
converted to zero capacity or unlimited limits. qhost's unavailable `-` remains
unknown. Per-job details cannot create unrequested or empty job IDs.

Running and pending job details share one parser. CPU/memory values and requested
time limits must match complete values, not numeric prefixes of corrupt output.
Duplicate job headers, duplicate resource fields, conflicting qconf limits and
successful detail responses missing requested job headers are errors. An absent
optional usage field remains unknown; it is not silently replaced by zero.
Job details are attached to the result only after all detail queries have been
processed successfully or have returned an explicitly handled command failure.
Repeated requested IDs are queried once; pending/absent lookups do not create
empty running-job records as a side effect of Perl hash dereferencing.

Cluster summaries recompute pending counters, rather than adding them on each
call. Hostname matching retains unique short-name matching but never merges two
different fully qualified domains just because their first labels match.

Important return-value units are:

* `queue_info`: CPU and wall limits are seconds.
* `jobs_info`: CPU/resource times are seconds; memory is kB.
* `users_info`: `freecpus` duration values are whole minutes, not seconds;
  duration zero means unlimited. A finite subminute limit advertises no available
  whole-minute slot, rather than becoming unlimited.
* `queue_info` / `nodes_info`: reserved slots are not free slots. These remain
  estimates, not promises that a job satisfies every scheduler resource/ACL rule.

The seconds/minutes assignment and newline-dependent line callback can both be
traced to repository commit `4d388c5f8f` (2008-09-09). For example, passing a
2,700-second queue limit directly into `freecpus` made ARC's GLUE publisher
advertise 2,700 minutes instead of 45. The callback also lost the final output
line whenever it lacked a newline. Both have explicit regression tests now.

Scanner snapshots with missing sections or job identities fail before accounting
lookups, deletion or completion. A qacct record needs a terminal timestamp,
failure status and numeric exit status; duplicate identity/status fields are
invalid. Failed/corrupt accounting uses the existing bounded accounting-lag retry
and wrapper-fallback policy, not a newly introduced indefinite wait. Malformed
wrapper exit codes produce unknown status, never an arbitrary status string.

Offline regression tests
------------------------

From the repository root, without configuring/building ARC or installing SGE:

    sh src/services/a-rex/lrms/sge/test/run-tests.sh

This runs the provider return-value fixtures, the table-driven `sge-failures.t`
suite and the real backend scripts against fake scheduler commands. It uses a
temporary directory and never contacts a live scheduler. Required Perl modules
are XML::Simple, Test::More and Test::Harness. A nonzero exit means failure.
For a configured build, `make check` in `infoproviders/test` and `lrms/sge/test`
also runs the registered tests. Set `SGE_TEST_KEEP=yes` to retain the outer test
workspace. The helper-script suite has its own temporary fixtures and cleanup.

Add parser regressions as named cases in `sge-failures.t`; add command/exit-status
fixtures in `sge.t` or `test/sge-lrms-regression.sh`. Existing cases cover signals,
nonzero exits with plausible stdout, executable paths containing spaces, literal
arguments, unterminated lines, wrong/missing XML sections, invalid numeric fields,
array ranges, reservations, duration units, partial qconf responses, malformed job
details, qacct corruption and bounded fallback. Tests use synthetic fixtures;
they are not captures from a production 2024 installation.

For a fast Perl-only reproduction of the reporting/process edge cases:

    prove -v -I src/services/a-rex/infoproviders src/services/a-rex/infoproviders/test/sge-reporting.t

Add a named corrupt-output case to that file's table to check both the error and
the invariant that the previous jobs result remains untouched. No generated
files, command simulator, live scheduler or build tree are needed for this test.

Reference and compatibility boundary
------------------------------------

Altair's official announcement confirms that Grid Engine 2024.1.0 is 8.9.0 and
explicitly discusses Alma 9/cgroups v2 support:
https://community.altair.com/discussion/33588/announcing-altair-grid-engine-2024-1-0-8-9-0
This establishes the release identity, not every command-output detail.

Altair's public User Guide documents separate reserved/used/total queue slots,
global pending jobs and qstat/qhost/qconf reporting:
https://help.altair.com/AltairGridEngine/UsersGuideGE.pdf (sections 3.1–3.3).
That URL currently serves the 2025.1.0 (8.10.0) edition, not the 2024 edition.
Public 2024 archive URLs could not be retrieved during this review. The existing
2024.1 / AGE 8.9.0 fixtures are retained as the target contract, but testing against
your exact Univa/Altair 2024 build still requires its manuals or anonymized command
captures (stdout, stderr, exit status and `qstat -help` banner). Do not interpret
offline tests or the newer manual as live 2024 compatibility certification.
